{
  // THE WORKER THIS CRATE BUILDS — and it is NOT DEPLOYED, and it is NOT `vale-gate`.
  //
  // `gateway/wrangler.jsonc` is the live console worker (`vale-gate`), and a real user is on it.
  // This file is the Rust half's own config, so that the crate can be built and measured without
  // touching the deployed script, its routes or its assets. The name is deliberately different from
  // `vale-gate`: `wrangler deploy` in this directory can create a NEW worker and can never
  // overwrite the console worker by accident.
  //
  // `workers_dev: false` and no `routes`: nothing on the internet can reach this worker until the
  // cutover, which is a separate decision with its own plan (a zone route on the console host is a
  // change to the live front door, not a step in this one).
  "name": "vale-gate-wasm",
  "main": "build/index.js",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-01",
  "workers_dev": false,
  "observability": { "enabled": false },
  "build": { "command": "worker-build --release" },
  // THE SAME DURABLE OBJECT THE TYPESCRIPT ROUTE READS. `script_name` is what makes this the SAME
  // BreakerDO instance the deployed console worker uses — without it, the binding would have to
  // name a class defined in THIS script, and the health card would be reporting a different
  // breaker's state. `reliability.ts`: `env.BREAKER.get(env.BREAKER.idFromName("og"))`.
  // **THE KV BINDING, AND WITHOUT IT THIS WORKER CANNOT ANSWER AT ALL — MEASURED 2026-10-07 ON REAL WORKERD.**
  //
  // `wrangler dev --remote` (the real runtime, the real bindings) listed what the script actually gets:
  //
  //     env.BREAKER (BreakerDO, defined in vale-gate)   Durable Object
  //     env.UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS ("120000")              Environment Variable
  //
  // — and **no `KEYS`**. Every request reads KV (the token, the user, the user's keys, `settings:US_PROXY`,
  // `providers:custom`), so every request died at the binding and the dev proxy reported it as
  // `Network connection lost`. **THE NODE HARNESS CANNOT SEE THIS**: `verify.mjs` injects a fake `KEYS`, so the
  // divergence sweep was 90/90 while the deployed script had nothing to read. The same class of gap as
  // `UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS` (see below), one binding over — and the same id as the console worker, because the
  // two must read the SAME KV: the records the console writes are what this worker routes on.
  "kv_namespaces": [{ "binding": "KEYS", "id": "a1fb10da621b411caeda6d98c060bbea" }],
  "durable_objects": {
    "bindings": [{ "name": "BREAKER", "class_name": "BreakerDO", "script_name": "vale-gate" }]
  },
  // **THE ONE VAR THE SHIPPING WORKER SETS AND THIS ONE DID NOT, AND IT IS NOT COSMETIC.** `vale-gate`
  // carries `UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS = 120000`; the crate's own default for it is **30 s**
  // (`reliability.rs`: `UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS = 30_000`), so without this line the cutover would cut every
  // non-og channel at 30 s where the shipping route allows 120 — a long-thinking request answered as a
  // timeout. Read through the API on 2026-10-06, when the rest of the two bindings were compared:
  //
  //     vale-gate       CONSOLE_HOST, UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS=120000, VISION_*, ACCESS_*, and 15 secrets
  //     vale-gate-wasm  none of those; 10 secrets
  //
  // **AND IT IS IN THE CONFIG RATHER THAN SET BY HAND, BECAUSE A HAND-SET VAR IS DROPPED BY THE NEXT
  // `wrangler deploy`** — this file is the record, and the deploy is what applies it.
  //
  // THE SECRETS THIS WORKER IS STILL MISSING ARE NAMED WHERE THE CUTOVER CAN ACT ON THEM: `AMD_API_KEY`,
  // `CMD_API_KEY`, `GMI_API_KEY` and `R4_API_KEY` are secrets on `vale-gate` and absent here, so those four
  // channels would answer the "not configured — add your own key in the console" config error for every user
  // without a key of their own. **A SECRET CANNOT BE READ BACK THROUGH THE API**, so this is the operator's
  // step (`wrangler secret put <NAME> --name vale-gate-wasm`), not a line in this file — and it is the
  // cutover's, not follow-up work.
  // **THE VARS THE PORTED DECISIONS READ, TAKEN FROM THE SHIPPING WORKER'S OWN CONFIG.** `wrangler.jsonc` in
  // `gateway/` is the live console worker's; this list is every var it sets that THIS crate reads, compared one
  // by one (its `ACCESS_*` three are the console's auth and are read nowhere in `src/` — checked, not assumed):
  //
  //     CONSOLE_HOST           the CORS stamp on every response — **AND IT IS NOT IN THIS FILE, DELIBERATELY**
  //     VISION_MODEL           `cm/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash` — NOT the crate's default (`og/mimo-v2.5`)
  //     VISION_CAPABLE_MODELS  the five-model allowlist — the crate's default is a different list
  //     UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS    120000, where the crate's own default is 30 s
  //
  // **`CONSOLE_HOST` IS THE ONE VALUE THAT CANNOT BE WRITTEN HERE.** `agent/tests/production_host.rs` is a
  // RATCHET over which files may name a production host, and its own refusal says why: "the list is a debt with
  // owners, not a permission: it may only shrink". Measured 2026-10-07 — putting the value in this file turned CI
  // red on `no_undeclared_file_names_a_production_host` (2 occurrences) — so the hostname stays in the ONE file
  // that already declares it (`gateway/wrangler.jsonc`) and `./scripts/build.sh gateway-wasm` reads it there and
  // passes it as `--var`. **A HAND-SET VAR WOULD BE DROPPED BY THE NEXT DEPLOY** (the same sentence this file
  // carries about `UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS`), which is why the deploy command is the thing that carries it.
  //
  // **EACH OF THE REMAINING THREE IS A DIVERGENCE IF IT IS MISSING, AND NONE OF THEM IS VISIBLE TO A HARNESS
  // THAT INJECTS ITS OWN ENV** — the sweep passes the vision vars explicitly, so it measured the decisions and
  // never the deployment. The secrets this worker still needs are named below.
  "vars": {
    "UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS": "120000",
    "VISION_MODEL": "cm/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash",
    "VISION_CAPABLE_MODELS": "deepseek-flash, cm/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash, qw/deepseek-v4.1-flash, r4/deepseek-v4.1-flash, og/mimo-v2.6-flash"
  }
  // `DO_AUTH` is a SECRET on the deployed worker (`wrangler secret put DO_AUTH`), not a var: it is
  // read here for the same reason `breakerHeaders` reads it — the DO refuses an unauthenticated
  // `/check`, and a refused read is `false`, which would report og healthy while its circuit is
  // open. The cutover must set it on this worker too.
}
